<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Boju</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">506 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Boju was fought in 506 BCE in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-helu-of-wu">King Helü of Wu</a>, advised by the Chu exile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zixu">Wu Zixu</a> and traditionally by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu">Sun Wu</a>, invaded Chu and defeated its main army. Wu forces then took the Chu capital Ying, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-zhao-of-chu">King Zhao of Chu</a> fled. Chu survived only after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> sent an army in response to the pleas of the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-baoxu">Shen Baoxu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-boju.jpg" alt="Battle of Boju" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Boju.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Wu was at first a minor state east of Chu, a major power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a> often at war with Jin to its north. To check Chu, Jin allied with Wu, trained its army and taught it to use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>. Wu first defeated Chu in 584 BCE, and in the next 70 years the two fought ten wars, most of them won by Wu. Wu Zixu fled to Wu after King Ping of Chu, urged by the official Fei Wuji, killed his father Wu She and brother Wu Shang, and he helped Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> take the throne as King Helü.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo Zhuan</a>, compiled in the 4th century BCE, gives the fullest account. In 506 BCE Helü, his brother Fugai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zixu">Wu Zixu</a> led the army, joined by the small states of Cai and Tang, up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a> and overland to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a>. The Chu commanders Nang Wa and Shen Yin Shu agreed that Shen would go north to Fangcheng to destroy the Wu boats and block the passes behind them, but after he left, Nang Wa, warned that Shen would take the credit, crossed the river and attacked at once.</p><p>After three defeats Chu drew up at Boju. Fugai disobeyed the king and attacked with his own 5,000 men, and the Chu army broke; Nang Wa fled to Zheng. Wu pursued, striking the Chu troops at the Qingfa River as half had crossed, and after five victories took Ying. Shen Yin Shu, returning, won at Yongshi, but wounded three times he had an officer kill him rather than be captured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 505 BCE Qin and Chu together defeated Wu in several battles. Fugai went home and declared himself king, forcing Helü to return and defeat him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-zhao-of-chu">King Zhao of Chu</a> came back to Ying. The historian Fan Wenlan counts the war the first large-scale war of the Eastern Zhou. Chu took long to recover, but Wu could not hold its gains and was itself conquered by Yue in 473 BCE. Sun Tzu's command rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s Shiji; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo Zhuan</a> does not mention him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Boju” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 506 BCE the State of Wu, advised by the Chu exile Wu Zixu, defeated Chu at <b>Boju</b> and occupied Ying; King Zhao fled, and the state survived only with help from the State of Qin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li></ul></section>
